Suppose some $g: B -> A$ satisfies $g compose f = id_A$. If $f(a) = f(overline(a))$, then $a = g(f(a)) = g(f(overline(a))) = overline(a)$, so $f$ must be injective. Conversely, let $f$ be injective: for $b in f(A)$ set $g(b) = a$ for the unique $a in A$ with $f(a) = b$, and for $b in.not f(A)$ choose $g(b)$ arbitrarily in $A$; then $g compose f = id_A$. For $f compose g = id_B$ as well, surjectivity is necessary because $f(g(B)) = B$ forces $f$ onto $B$, and sufficient because each $b in B$ equals $f(a)$ for some $a in A$, so taking $g(b) = a$ gives $f compose g = id_B$. The construction of $g$ is consistent across the two parts, so the result follows.
